$BuildinPages{"Habitat/NewSyntax"} = <<'HABITAT_NEWFEATURES';
= Advanced features =
<toc>

The basics are on [[Habitat/Markup]]. This page shows what makes Habitat
different: pages that reuse other pages, pages that carry their own
formatting rules, Markdown, and blogs and forums. The examples use small
demo pages under <tt>test/</tt>.

== # Embedding pages ==

!! You type !! You get !!
|| <tt><nowiki>{{test/demo1}}</nowiki></tt> || page [[test/demo1]], rendered, inside this page ||
|| <tt><nowiki>{(test/demo1)}</nowiki></tt> || the raw text of [[test/demo1]], formatted as part of this page ||

Here is <tt><nowiki>{{test/demo1}}</nowiki></tt>:
{{test/demo1}}
and here is its source, shown with <tt><nowiki><pre>{(test/demo1)}</pre></nowiki></tt>:
<pre>{(test/demo1)}</pre>

Embedded pages can embed others in turn ([[test/demo2]] embeds
[[test/demo1]]), up to <tt>$MaxEmbedDepth</tt> levels. Use this for
headers, footers, menus, or any text that should appear on many pages but
be edited in one place. Each embedded page keeps its own permissions: a
reader who may not see it gets nothing.

Add <tt>raw=1</tt> to any page address to get its plain text, for example
[http:?action=browse&id=test/demo3&raw=1 test/demo3 as text].

== # Page rules ==

A page can carry text-rewriting rules in hidden companion pages:

!! Rule page !! Applied !!
|| <tt>Page/.v0</tt> || when the page is shown, before the wiki markup is converted ||
|| <tt>Page/.v1</tt> || when the page is shown, after it has become HTML ||
|| <tt>Page/.e0</tt> || when the edit form is opened ||
|| <tt>Page/.e1</tt> || when an edit is saved ||

'''Rules are inherited:''' the rules of <tt>Page</tt> also apply to
<tt>Page/Sub</tt> and every page below it, before the subpage's own. One
set of rules can so give a whole section of the wiki its own format: a bug
tracker, a glossary, a slide deck. Only administrators can edit rule pages.

Each line of a rule page is one rule:

!! Rule !! Effect !!
|| <tt>from/to</tt> || replace the regular expression <tt>from</tt> with <tt>to</tt> (add <tt>/g</tt>, <tt>/i</tt> or <tt>/m</tt> for the usual flags) ||
|| <tt>GREP pattern</tt> || keep only the lines matching <tt>pattern</tt> ||
|| <tt>HEAD n</tt>, <tt>TAIL n</tt> || keep only the first or last <tt>n</tt> lines ||

A backslash at the end of a line continues the rule on the next line.
Rule output may contain placeholders, which are filled in when the page is
shown: <tt><nowiki>&amp;lt;pagename&amp;gt;</nowiki></tt> (the last part of the
page name), <tt><nowiki>&amp;lt;fullpagename&amp;gt;</nowiki></tt>,
<tt><nowiki>&amp;lt;date&amp;gt;</nowiki></tt> and <tt><nowiki>&amp;lt;time&amp;gt;</nowiki></tt>
(when the page was created), and <tt><nowiki>&amp;lt;datenow&amp;gt;</nowiki></tt> and
<tt><nowiki>&amp;lt;timenow&amp;gt;</nowiki></tt>. In page text they are written with
the HTML escapes exactly as shown here.

'''Rules in YAML.''' A rule page whose first line (after any blank or
<tt>#</tt> comment lines) is <tt>---</tt> or starts with <tt>- </tt> is read
as a YAML list instead, one entry per rule, applied in order:

!! Entry !! Effect !!
|| <tt>from:</tt> regex, <tt>to:</tt> text, <tt>opt:</tt> flags || replace; <tt>\1</tt> or <tt>$1</tt> in <tt>to</tt> is the first group; <tt>opt</tt> is any of <tt>gimsx</tt> and defaults to <tt>g</tt> ||
|| <tt>grep:</tt> regex || keep only the lines that match ||
|| <tt>head:</tt> n, <tt>tail:</tt> n || keep only the first or last <tt>n</tt> lines ||
|| <tt>prepend:</tt> text, <tt>append:</tt> text || add text at the start or the end, as written ||

Write regular expressions in single quotes, so backslashes stay as typed.
For replacement text over several lines, write <tt>to: |</tt> (or
<tt>to: |-</tt>, which drops the final newline) and indent the lines below
it deeper than <tt>to:</tt>. The first line sets the indent; deeper
indentation and blank lines are kept. <tt>|2-</tt> sets the indent to 2
explicitly, for a block whose first line is indented further than the rest.
Each entry must use the block form shown here; the one-line
<tt><nowiki>{from: a, to: b}</nowiki></tt> form is not supported. Rules are
regular-expression replacements only; nothing in them is run as code.

[[test/card/.v0]] turns every page under <tt>test/card</tt> into a table:
<pre>{(test/card/.v0)}</pre>
so [[test/card/alice]], whose text is
<pre>{(test/card/alice)}</pre>
is shown as
{{test/card/alice}}

Both formats can be mixed: a page inherits line rules from one ancestor
and YAML rules from another.

For example, [[test/code/.v0]] holds two rules that wrap the text of every
page under <tt>test/code</tt> in <tt><nowiki><pre></nowiki></tt>:
<pre>{(test/code/.v0)}</pre>
so [[test/code/codedemo]], whose text is
<pre>{(test/code/codedemo)}</pre>
is shown as
{{test/code/codedemo}}

The site configuration can also attach rules, or a Perl function, to page
names by pattern (<tt>%NameSpaceV0</tt> and friends, <tt>%ExtViewer</tt>);
[[test/demofun1]] is produced by such a function.

== # Markdown pages ==

A page whose first line is
<pre><nowiki><!-- markdown --></nowiki></pre>
is written in Markdown instead of wiki markup (this needs the
<tt>Text::Markdown::Discount</tt> Perl module). As everywhere, raw HTML in
it is cleaned unless an administrator saved the page.

== # Blogs and forums ==

Any page becomes a ''board'' when an '''administrator''' saves it with a
line like one of these:
<pre><nowiki><board type=forum perpage=20 post=user reply=user>
<board type=blog perpage=10 post=editor reply=user></nowiki></pre>
That line is replaced by the list of posts, with a ''New post'' button for
those allowed to write.

!! Setting !! Values !! Forum default !! Blog default !!
|| <tt>type</tt> || <tt>forum</tt> or <tt>blog</tt> || || ||
|| <tt>perpage</tt> || posts (and replies) per page, 1-200 || 20 || 10 ||
|| <tt>post</tt> || who may start a post: <tt>anon</tt>, <tt>user</tt>, <tt>editor</tt>, <tt>admin</tt> || <tt>user</tt> || <tt>editor</tt> ||
|| <tt>reply</tt> || who may reply, same values || <tt>user</tt> || <tt>user</tt> ||

* Every post and every reply is a wiki page of its own, with history, diff and edit link. Forum topics are named <tt>Forum/0001</tt>, blog posts after the date and title (<tt>Blog/2026-11-07-Hello-World</tt>), and replies <tt>Forum/0001/0001</tt>.
* A forum lists topics by latest activity, with reply counts; a blog shows the newest posts first with an excerpt. Both page through long lists and can be filtered by tag.
* Authors can edit their own posts; editors and administrators can also make a topic sticky, close it to replies, and delete replies or whole threads.
* Only an administrator's save switches a board on or changes its settings, so nobody else can open a board to anonymous posting.

The full guide is <tt>BOARDS.md</tt> in the source folder.

== # Who may read and edit what ==

Set in the site configuration:

!! Setting !! Effect !!
|| <tt>%Permissions</tt> || page-name patterns that need a higher level to read ||
|| <tt>%EditPermissions</tt> || page-name patterns that need a higher level to edit ||
|| <tt>$PermEditAllowed</tt> || the lowest level that may edit at all ||
|| <tt>$RegistrationPermission</tt> || who may create an account ||

Levels are: anonymous visitor, logged-in user, editor and administrator.
Administrators can also lock single pages, or the whole site, from the
administration links.
